Report Snapshot
Generative AI is transforming discovery and forcing marketers to rethink visibility. Success now depends on treating AI as a 
branding channel, managing generative engine optimization (GEO) separately from SEO, and adapting fast as AI models evolve.
Key Question: How is GEO different from SEO, and how should brands respond?
Key Stat: Fewer than 10% of the sources cited in ChatGPT, Gemini, and Copilot rank in the top 10 Google organic search results 
for the same query—meaning SEO tactics won’t guarantee brands visibility in AI.

Page 4Copyright © 2025, EMARKETER Inc. All rights reserved.
 ■
AI offers brands an attractive audience. More than a billion people use AI, and most of them use it for discovery. AI also 
drives product discovery even when people aren’t asking about products. Best of all for marketers, higher-income consumers 
are most likely to use AI daily.
 ■
SEO tactics don’t guarantee visibility in AI. Nearly everyone—from established SEO experts to AI boosters—recommends 
a standard set of modern SEO best practices to improve AI visibility. But fewer than 10% of the sources cited in ChatGPT, 
Gemini, and Copilot rank in the top 10 Google organic search results for the same query.
 ■
To succeed, optimize for and measure GEO differently than SEO. No one has solved GEO, and things are sure to change 
quickly. But brands appear most likely to succeed if they treat generative engines as a branding channel; separate on-site 
GEO from on-site SEO; and target their off-site GEO efforts to public forums and review sites.
 
Definitions
Generative engines are AI tools that respond to user prompts by generating content in the form of text, images, audio, or video. The most 
popular generative engines right now include ChatGPT, Google Gemini, Perplexity, and Claude.
Generative engine optimization (GEO) describes a set of tactics brands use to increase their chances of appearing in generative 
engine answers.
Search engine optimization (SEO) describes a set of tactics brands use to increase their chances of appearing in search engine results.
Black hat SEO describes a set of deceptive tactics that manipulate search engines into preferring content that’s not most useful 
for searchers.
Experience, Expertise, Authoritativeness, Trustworthiness (E-E-A-T) is a framework Google uses to explain how its search engine 
evaluates content; marketers have also adopted this model to guide their SEO efforts.
Schema markup is a way of structuring metadata to help search engines understand a webpage’s content, author, date of publication, 
and other information.
Executive Summary
Generative Engine Optimization in 2026

Page 5Copyright © 2025, EMARKETER Inc. All rights reserved.
AI offers brands an attractive and 
trusting audience
More than a billion people use AI—mostly for the same 
reason they use search. In October 2025, OpenAI said 
that 800 million people worldwide use ChatGPT weekly; 
hundreds of millions more use competing generative 
engines. And 60% of US adults have used AI to search for 
information, according to a July 2025 Associated Press-
NORC survey.
AI drives product discovery even when people don’t 
ask about products. OpenAI acknowledged that, as of 
June 2025, just 2.1% of ChatGPT user prompts mentioned 
“purchasable products.” But generative engines often tell 
users about products anyway: AI made unprompted product 
recommendations in about one-third of non-shopping 
conversations studied by Profound, per an October 
2025 study.
Many high-value consumers trust AI more than search. 
Nine in 10 regular AI users trust responses from generative 
engines as much as or more than they trust organic search 
results, per a January 2025 Attest survey. And these AI fans 
are the customers brands want to reach: Higher-income 
consumers are twice as likely as lower-income consumers to 
use AI daily, according to a June 2025 Menlo Ventures study.
SEO tactics don’t guarantee 
visibility in AI
Nearly everyone claims effective SEO will drive AI 
visibility. Most search experts say that “good SEO is good 
GEO.” Most AI boosters, stuck on a dated vision of black 
hat SEO, say it’s time to discard the old playbook and 
start over—before prescribing something that looks a lot 
like modern SEO best practices. Ultimately, both groups 
recommend optimizing for AI using standard strategies 
like good schema markup and a focus on Google’s E-E-
A-T framework.
But while good SEO may be good GEO, it’s rarely great 
GEO. Fewer than 10% of the sources cited in ChatGPT, 
Gemini, and Copilot rank in the top 10 Google organic 
search results for the same query, according to an August 
2025 Ahrefs study. (Even a more optimistic June 2025 
Semrush study says just over one-quarter of ChatGPT 
citations rank on Google’s first page.) And most of the links 
featured in Google AI Overviews don’t appear on the first 
page of Google results either, according to August 2025 
data from Writesonic.

Page 6Copyright © 2025, EMARKETER Inc. All rights reserved.
No one has GEO all figured out. Consumers have had 
access to generative engines for fewer than three years. The 
smartest people in AI are the first to remind us that we’re 
early in this industry and we all have a lot to learn. While 
some vendors are making great progress in decoding AI 
visibility, anyone who says they have all the answers is either 
wildly overconfident or simply misguided.
GEO best practices are guaranteed to change quickly. 
Just as Google Search releases several core updates each 
year, AI tools also constantly evolve. OpenAI has released 
about a dozen new models since 2022, each changing how 
ChatGPT processes information and displays it to users. The 
brands that win in GEO will be the ones that move as quickly 
as the AI models.
To succeed, optimize for and 
measure GEO differently than SEO
Brands must differentiate their GEO and SEO 
strategies. Marketers hoping to tap into AI discovery may 
feel like every AI data source provides different numbers 
and every new piece of GEO advice contradicts the last. 
But after studying how AI discovery differs from traditional 
search and sifting fact from fiction, we believe brands must 
adapt to AI-driven discovery in three ways.
1. Treat AI as a brand channel
AI discovery can shape the customer journey before 
it even begins. While AI users rarely ask about products, 
28.8% of ChatGPT prompts seek “practical guidance,” 
according to June 2025 OpenAI data. By reaching users at 
this point of a conversation—when they describe a problem 
or need—brands can influence how customers think about 
an entire product category.
But people are less likely to click on or convert from AI 
results. Users clicked through on just 5.7% of ChatGPT 
responses in June 2025, per Sensor Tower. Even when 
AI does drive consumers to retailer sites, those visits 
generate 27% less revenue than non-AI visits, per July 2025 
Adobe data.
Optimize for and measure AI as a brand channel, 
not a sales channel. AI discovery’s position early in the 
customer journey offers marketers a chance to build their 
brand before consumers are even in-market. To succeed, 
brands must optimize webpages not just for their product 
categories, but for the consumer needs that drive people to 
those categories.

Page 7Copyright © 2025, EMARKETER Inc. All rights reserved.
2. Manage on-site GEO separately 
from SEO
Build separate pages for AI. As AI discovery grows, 
marketers need three sets of site content: one for direct 
traffic, another for search engines, and a third for generative 
engines. Fintech vendor Ramp, already successful at SEO, 
has also built thousands of AI-targeted pages optimized to 
describe human needs rather than its own products—and 
saw AI visibility increase by 24.6% for its target prompts.
Measure GEO separately from SEO. Because AI drives 
less response than search, brands should measure both 
mentions and the brand impact generated by those AI 
mentions. Brands that track AI traffic should report on it 
separately from search traffic: Lily Ray, vice president of 
SEO strategy and research at Amsive, says she creates 
new segments in her clients’ web analytics platforms for AI-
driven visits.
3. Target off-site GEO efforts to 
public forums
Different AIs cite different sources, but they all love 
community content. Only one of ChatGPT’s top three 
sources is also in Gemini’s or Perplexity’s top three, per 
Profound. But one of the few things AI engines agree on is 
a reliance on public forums and review sites. In June 2025, 
Ahrefs and Semrush both cited Reddit, Wikipedia, and 
YouTube as the top three AI sources.
Activate brand fans on Reddit. While sources like 
Promptwatch report that Reddit’s dominance as an AI 
source declined in September 2025, they also acknowledge 
that it remains ChatGPT’s top-cited domain. That’s why 
automaker Skoda asked members of the r/CarTalkUK 
subreddit to select features for a special “Redditor edition” 
of one model. The result: Website visits grew 44%, and 
Skoda sold 2.5 times more special-edition cars than 
planned, per Reddit.
Get involved in category-specific community sites as 
well. There’s no guarantee generative engines will always 
prefer Reddit and YouTube. Even today, they don’t only 
cite the largest forums; review-heavy sites like TechRadar, 
TripAdvisor, Yelp, and NerdWallet also feature regularly. 
Cloud providers should engage in GitHub discussions; 
lawncare marketers should promote their offerings on 
Nextdoor; and any brand whose product category generates 
lots of Quora questions should jump in to respond.
Adapting to AI discovery may 
prove simpler for B2B brands
Early evidence suggests B2B marketers will have an 
easier time with AI. Most of the data cited in this report 
focuses on either general user behavior or B2C-specific 
categories. But some small-scale studies show a much 
stronger overlap between generative engine and search 
engine citations for some B2B categories, and a handful 
of case studies show higher B2B conversion rates from 
generative engines than from search. Therefore, B2B brands 
should take the advice in this report with a grain of salt and 
keep an eye out for reliable studies in their industries.

